From: piano-linux bring-up <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ui/keyboard: open the OSK when it is enabled while a text
 input already has focus

On a Wayland session the on-screen keyboard is opened only by the input
controller's panel-state notification.  KeyboardManager._syncEnabled() merely
constructs the Keyboard actor, and the focus-changed -> open() path in
Keyboard._init() is guarded by `!Meta.is_wayland_compositor()`, so it never
runs here.

With the project default screen-keyboard-enabled=false, the actor exists only
while the seat reports touch mode AND the last input device was a touchscreen.
Tap a phone-number field at the moment any other input device is "last" (the
pen tablet-tool uinput that piano-touch-input --pen-input creates, a keyboard
touchpad, a paired Bluetooth mouse) and `enabled` is false, `_keyboard` is
null, and the panel-state notification mutter sends for that focus arrives
nowhere.  It is not replayed.  Result: no keyboard.

The next text commit — pasting a number, typing — makes mutter notify again.
By then a touch event has turned touch mode on and _syncEnabled() has built
the actor, so the notification finally has a listener and the keyboard shows
up (with the phone layout, which is why the layout itself was never the
problem).

Fix: when _syncEnabled() creates the actor and a text input already has
focus, open it, doing what the missed notification would have done.  The call
mirrors _onKeyboardStateChanged(), including its argument.  Where the
auto-detection was already enabled the actor exists, this branch is not
reached, and behaviour is unchanged.

Applies to upstream GNOME Shell 48 (gnome-48 branch), tested against
js/ui/keyboard.js at the head of that branch.
Forwarded: no

--- a/js/ui/keyboard.js
+++ b/js/ui/keyboard.js
@@ -1114,6 +1114,21 @@ export class KeyboardManager extends Signals.EventEmitter {
                 this.emit('visibility-changed');
                 this._bottomDragAction.enabled = !this._keyboard.visible;
             });
+
+            /* Nothing here opens the keyboard.  On Wayland the only thing that
+             * does is the input controller's panel-state notification
+             * (_onKeyboardStateChanged); the focus-changed path right above is
+             * explicitly X11-only.  So if this object did not exist yet when
+             * the text field got focus — because the seat was not in touch
+             * mode, or the last input device was not the touchscreen — that
+             * notification went nowhere and is never replayed.  Creating the
+             * keyboard now leaves a focused field with no keyboard until the
+             * client commits its text again (a paste, a keystroke), which is
+             * exactly the "tap the phone-number field, no keyboard; paste,
+             * and it appears" symptom.  Mirror what panel-state would have
+             * done, including the (truthy) argument upstream passes: */
+            if (Main.inputMethod?.currentFocus)
+                this._keyboard.open(Main.layoutManager.focusIndex);
         } else if (!enabled && this._keyboard) {
             this._keyboard.setCursorLocation(null);
             this._keyboard.destroy();
